-- title:
--   Coordinate ring of an elliptic curve is Dedekind
-- statement:
--   Let $K$ be an algebraically closed field and let $W$ be a Weierstrass curve over $K$, that is, a tuple of coefficients $(a_1,a_2,a_3,a_4,a_6)$ defining the equation $Y^2 + a_1XY + a_3Y = X^3 + a_2X^2 + a_4X + a_6$, subject to the hypothesis `W.IsElliptic`, i.e. the discriminant $\Delta$ of $W$ is a unit of $K$, hence nonzero. The assertion is that the affine coordinate ring of $W$, namely the quotient of the bivariate polynomial ring $K[X][Y]$ by the principal ideal generated by the Weierstrass polynomial $Y^2 + a_1XY + a_3Y - (X^3 + a_2X^2 + a_4X + a_6)$, is a Dedekind domain: it is an integral domain which is Noetherian, of Krull dimension at most one, and integrally closed in its fraction field. No further hypotheses are imposed; in particular the characteristic of $K$ is arbitrary.
--
--   This is the ring-theoretic form of the statement that a nonsingular affine plane curve has Dedekind coordinate ring, specialised to Weierstrass cubics with nonvanishing discriminant over an algebraically closed field. It makes the height-one spectrum and the associated valuations available on the coordinate ring, which is the basis for orders of vanishing and divisors of rational functions on the curve, and it is used throughout the treatment of torsion, the Weil pairing and the geometry of modular curves.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_WeierstrassCurve_Affine_CoordinateRing_isDedekindDomain.lean

